### 2.2 The cache struct

```rust
pub struct LruCache<K, V> {
    capacity: usize,
    map: HashMap<K, usize>,      // key -> node index
    nodes: Vec<Node<K, V>>,      // arena of nodes
    head: Option<usize>,         // most-recently-used
    tail: Option<usize>,         // least-recently-used
    free: Vec<usize>,            // recycled indices
}
```

Why an arena? Because `Vec<Node>` owns all nodes contiguously. Links are indices, so no aliasing, no `Rc`, no `unsafe`. When we evict, we don't shrink the `Vec`; we push the index onto `free` for reuse. This keeps allocation churn low.

### 2.3 Constructor

```rust
impl<K: Eq + Hash, V> LruCache<K, V> {
    pub fn new(capacity: usize) -> Self {
        assert!(capacity > 0, "capacity must be > 0");
        Self {
            capacity,
            map: HashMap::with_capacity(capacity),
            nodes: Vec::with_capacity(capacity),
            head: None,
            tail: None,
            free: Vec::new(),
        }
    }
}
```

We assert capacity > 0 because a zero-capacity cache is meaningless and would cause subtle bugs in eviction logic. If you prefer graceful handling, return a `Result` instead — see §4.

